  <p>
    Acne happens when a follicle becomes blocked with oil and dead skin cells, bacteria multiply
    inside it, and the wall of the follicle inflames. Hormones drive how much oil the gland makes,
    which is why it starts at puberty and why it often persists or returns in adult women.
  </p>

  <p>
    It is not caused by being unclean. Scrubbing harder makes it worse, because the barrier damage
    adds inflammation to a follicle that is already inflamed.
  </p>

  <h2>What type you have changes the treatment</h2>

  <h3>Comedonal</h3>
  <p>
    Blackheads and whiteheads, with little redness. Responds to topical retinoids. Antibiotics do
    very little here, which is why they are often the wrong first prescription.
  </p>

  <h3>Inflammatory</h3>
  <p>
    Red papules and pustules. Usually needs a retinoid combined with benzoyl peroxide or a topical
    antibiotic, and sometimes a short oral antibiotic course.
  </p>

  <h3>Nodulocystic</h3>
  <p>
    Deep, painful lumps that last for weeks and scar. This is the type where delaying effective
    treatment costs permanent texture change, and where isotretinoin is usually the right answer
    rather than the last resort.
  </p>

  <h3>Hormonal pattern</h3>
  <p>
    Acne along the jawline and chin in adult women, often worse before periods. Worth checking for
    PCOS when it comes with irregular cycles, excess facial hair or hair thinning. Treatment may
    include anti-androgens rather than more antibiotics.
  </p>

  <h2>How it is assessed</h2>

  <p>
    The examination establishes the type, the depth, whether scarring has already started, and
    whether anything is driving it — a steroid cream, a hair oil, a supplement, or a hormonal
    cause. The history matters as much as the appearance: what has already been used, for how long,
    and what happened.
  </p>

  <p>
    Blood tests are not routine. They are ordered where the pattern suggests a hormonal cause, and
    before starting isotretinoin.
  </p>

  <h2>Treatments with real evidence</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Topical retinoids</strong> — adapalene, tretinoin. The backbone of almost every plan. Expect dryness and some irritation for the first few weeks, and expect it to settle.</li>
    <li><strong>Benzoyl peroxide</strong> — reduces bacteria without producing resistance, which is why it is usually paired with any antibiotic.</li>
    <li><strong>Topical and oral antibiotics</strong> — useful for inflammatory acne, but for a limited course. Long unbroken antibiotic use is how resistance develops.</li>
    <li><strong>Anti-androgens</strong> — for hormonal pattern acne in women, where oil production is the driver.</li>
    <li><strong>Isotretinoin</strong> — for severe, scarring or treatment-resistant acne. Highly effective, and requires monitoring and counselling.</li>
    <li><strong>Chemical peels</strong> — salicylic acid peels help comedonal acne and post-acne marks as an addition, not as the whole plan.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="note note-warn">
    <h3>What to be cautious about</h3>
    <p>
      Steroid-containing creams sold over the counter for "pimples and fairness" clear acne for a
      week and then make it considerably worse, often with permanent redness and broken vessels.
      This is one of the most common avoidable problems seen in clinic. If a cream worked
      dramatically in a few days and then stopped, bring the tube to your consultation.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    Facials, frequent scrubbing and most "anti-acne" face washes do very little on their own. They
    are not harmful in moderation, but they are not treatment, and months spent on them are months
    in which nodular acne is scarring.
  </p>
